{
  "requirement": "A node's structural hash MUST be the SHA-256 digest of an unambiguous encoding of\nits outline-relevant structure, including siblings its profile attaches to it:\nthe kinds of its named grammar nodes and, in source order, its text pieces. A\ntext piece MUST be the text of a leaf token, or text inside a node that no child\ntoken covers. Each text piece MUST be hashed as its ordered list of words, where\nwords are separated by whitespace, the whitespace itself, including leading and\ntrailing whitespace, is never hashed, and each word is encoded separately, so\n`ab cd` and `abcd` differ. The same rule MUST apply in every language. Comments\nMUST be excluded, except comment kinds the profile includes; the Rust profile MUST include doc comments. For an included comment, only its text and its\nprofile's comment group MUST be hashed, not its delimiter tokens such as `//`,\n`///`, `//!`, `/*`, `*/`, or a leading `*`; the Rust profile's groups are inner\nand outer doc comments. The words of consecutive included comments of one group\nMUST be hashed as one ordered list, so reflowing them across more or fewer lines\nor comments does not change the hash, while moving text between groups does. A `line`\nnode's hash MUST be computed by the same word rule over the line's text. A node's\nidentity hash MUST be computed the same way but MUST also exclude its outline\ndescendants and their attached siblings, so editing a descendant does not change\nits ancestors' selectors.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.structural_hash`, and their assertions enforce the expected outcomes. I did not request implementation source. The only thin spot is a minor one, noted at the end.\n\n**SHA-256 of an unambiguous encoding, with kinds of named grammar nodes**\n- `hashes_include_grammar_kinds_nesting_and_sha256_digests` checks that identical text `x;` gives different hashes under the Rust and Python grammars, so node kinds are hashed.\n- The same test shows that nested and sibling `Encoder` sequences differ, which covers structure.\n- It also asserts the standard SHA-256 empty vector and a known digest for the one-word line `hello`. These pin the digest algorithm and the tagged, length-prefixed word encoding.\n\n**Text pieces hashed as ordered words; whitespace never hashed; `ab cd` differs from `abcd`**\n- `prose_words_are_encoded_separately`, `editing_a_doc_comment_word_changes_the_hash`, `string_literals_hash_as_words`, `plain_text_line_hashes_ignore_whitespace_but_not_word_breaks` and `markdown_hashes_include_paragraph_text_between_punctuation_tokens` cover this.\n- They cover leading, trailing, internal and multiple whitespace, and tabs. Each also asserts that removing a word break changes the hash.\n- Leaf-token text is covered by string literals in Rust, TOML and YAML block scalars.\n- Text inside a node that no child token covers is covered by the Markdown paragraph tests.\n\n**Same rule in every language**\n- Cases span Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, YAML and plain text. The Rust and Python cases are in `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` and `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing`. The TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML cases are in `comments_are_excluded_from_hashes_in_every_other_profile`.\n\n**Comments excluded, except the Rust profile's doc comments**\n- `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` shows that a plain `//` comment can change without changing the hash, while doc text, attributes and code edits do change it.\n- `comments_are_excluded_from_hashes_in_every_other_profile` shows that comment edits and removals leave the hash unchanged in Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML. In each language a non-comment edit does change it.\n\n**Included comments: delimiters not hashed, group-based list, reflow-stable**\n- `reflowing_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` covers `///` and `/** * */` forms, extra spaces and blank doc lines.\n- `reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` covers `//!`, `/*! */` and `/** */`, and shows `/** */` equals `///`.\n- `inner_and_outer_doc_comments_hash_in_separate_groups` asserts that moving text between the inner and outer groups changes the hash, while splitting within one group does not.\n\n**`line` node hash**\n- The hash of a plain-text line equals `hash::line`, matches the known `hello` vector, and is whitespace-insensitive.\n\n**Identity hash**\n- `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants` covers Rust. Editing or removing a descendant's doc comment or attribute changes the ancestor's structural hash but not its identity hash. A node's own doc comment or attribute changes its identity hash. A node without descendants has identity equal to structural.\n- `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing` covers Python decorators the same way, for both a definition and an enclosing class.\n- `anonymous_selectors_use_identity_hashes` and `outline_inputs_survive_moves_and_unhashed_comments_but_not_edits` check the identity and structural hashes end to end.\n- The test in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/redaction/tests.rs` and the repository-level test in `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs` add supporting coverage.\n\n**Minor gap**\nNo test directly compares the encodings of different word-boundary splits, such as `a bc` against `ab c`. Length prefixing is pinned only by the single-word `hello` vector, and `ab cd` against `abcd` is covered. I judge this too minor to count as a gap.",
  "references": "98fe3a27169505d6b8354507f721f459e4ebb224ec2190b92357a924e0d91645",
